  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_supply",
      "components": ["U4", "C36"],
      "pins": ["U4.8"],
      "nets": ["+5V (flash VCC net)"],
      "observed": "On the 'FCC Peripherals' sheet the Winbond W25N01GVZEIG_TR NAND flash U4 has its VCC pin (pin 8) tied to a 5 V net (the label above the pin reads as a 5 V rail, distinct from the +3.3V labels used on the same sheet for R24/R25 and the EEPROM/PWM-driver VCC pins), with only C36 100 nF as decoupling.",
      "expected": "The W25N01GV is a 3.3 V-only part (VCC 2.7-3.6 V) and, per the design's own distribution scheme, should be powered from the 3V3 rail like the other FCC digital peripherals; the 5 V rail is reserved for servo/PWM power, the GPS and the CH330 bridges.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "'U4 W25N01GVZEIG_TR ... VCC 8' connected to the 5 V net (FCC Peripherals sheet)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "'3V3 feeds both MCUs (FCC, NAVC) and all I2C sensor ICs; 5 V feeds the servo/PWM connector bank, the GPS module, and the CH330 USB-UART bridges.'"}
      ],
      "impact": "Applying 5 V to the 3.3 V flash exceeds its absolute-maximum supply, likely destroying U4 (and/or latching up), so the FCC loses its NAND flash storage.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design is largely consistent with the specification, but the FCC-side W25N01GV flash (U4) appears to have its VCC pin tied to a 5 V net rather than the 3.3 V rail, which would over-voltage the 3.3 V-only NAND part."
}

